{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793986400,"nonce":"CTrEPREnZ4QEXMdwNaY/czbVWks=","title":"use_state_keyed and use_signal_keyed deadlock when the initial value creates a signal","message":"use_state_keyed and use_signal_keyed evaluated the caller's init closure while holding the reactive graph lock (and the hooks lock). TextInputData::new creates its revision and focus signals through the process-wide graph, which is the same graph the context holds, so ctx.use_state_keyed(key, || text_input_state_with_placeholder(..)) waits on its own lock forever on first build. A closure that calls another keyed hook deadlocks on the hooks lock the same way.\n\nFound running mobile/example on an Android emulator: the app logged nothing after the first layout and Android killed it with \"Input dispatching timed out\"; the ANR thread dump placed android_main inside keyboard_section -\u003e use_state_keyed -\u003e text_input_data_with_placeholder. It is not Android specific; it follows any use_state_keyed whose init builds a text input, on every platform. It surfaced after the text input began creating its own revision and focus signals.\n\nFix: both hooks look the key up, run init with no lock held, then take the locks to insert, keeping the first value if another caller got there first. Two regression tests (initial value creates a signal; initial value calls another hook) deadlock on the old code and pass on the new.\n\nThis also explains why the mobile example showed a black screen and an unresponsive app in the emulator runs for 4e110cb, so that issue's resume checks could not start until this was fixed.","files":null}]}